[Testing] These are async gauges, so temporality doesn't rescue this: the SDK drops a series that records no measurement in a collection cycle even under cumulative temporality (`CumulativeAsynchronousMetricStorage#doCollect` — "If they stop reporting a measurement for a collection, the series ends"). And `MetricsVerifier.verify` requires every expected metric in a single export request (`verifyAllExpectedMetricsWereReceived` runs regardless of strict mode). So a compaction has to be in flight during one of the 5s export cycles.

~20s of throttled compaction gives about four such cycles, so the window itself is comfortable. The residual risk is the one-shot `nodetool compact` — it is fired asynchronously right before the await and its failure is swallowed.

Looping it until verification finishes would make this deterministic; a major compaction rewrites the SSTable on every pass, so each iteration is another ~20s window:

``java
private static final AtomicBoolean keepCompacting = new AtomicBoolean(true);
// background thread: while (keepCompacting.get()) { nodetool(target, "compact", "--", "test", "data"); }
// after verifyMetrics(...): keepCompacting.set(false);
``

That would also let you shrink the seed volume and cut the `cqlsh COPY` time.

